AP040 differential testing against QEMU
=======================================

run_diff.sh generates random integer programs (gen_diff.py), runs the
same flat binary on the AP040 (verilated tb_ap040_program) and on
qemu-system-m68k -M virt -cpu m68040, and compares the $3000-$3FFF
memory window: sandbox data, the final register file image and the
final CCR.

The per-instruction CCR trace ($3800-$3EFF) is generated but NOT
compared by default. QEMU 11.0.2's m68k lazy flag evaluation was found
to produce flags that contradict the M68000PRM (and QEMU's own behavior
in isolated sequences) in at least these shapes, all reproduced in this
directory during bring-up:

  - SUBX with X=0, Z(old)=0 and result 0 reports Z=1 when the previous
    flag producer was a lazily evaluated ALU op ("work/qbug.s" repro:
    PRM says Z unchanged, i.e. 0)
  - NEGX C reported 0 for a nonzero result when preceded by a bitfield
    instruction with a register offset (stream context only)
  - MULU.L overflow V lost in similar stream contexts, although the
    isolated case ("work/mulv.s") is handled correctly

The AP040 side of each arbitration was confirmed against hand-computed
PRM semantics ("work/truth.s"). AP040 flag correctness is regression
tested by tests/ap040/asm/* with exact CCR expectations; the
differential run verifies data paths, EA computation and instruction
sequencing, where QEMU is reliable.

Usage:
  verilator --binary --timing ... tb_ap040_program.v rtl/ap040/*.v
  SIM=/path/to/Vtb_ap040_program ./run_diff.sh 1 50 150

Floating-point differential (gen_fpdiff.py / run_fpdiff.sh)
----------------------------------------------------------
Random FP programs: a pool of normalized extended/single/double constants,
FP0-FP7 loaded from it, then a stream of FADD/FSUB/FMUL/FDIV/FSQRT/FABS/
FNEG/FMOVE (all formats, register and memory) with FPCR rounding changes,
ending in FMOVEM.X of all eight registers plus FPCR and the FPSR condition
codes into the compared window.  Operand ranges are constrained so no
operation overflows, underflows, denormalizes, or produces Inf/NaN: those
are exactly the cases where AP040 (which traps unsupported data types to
vector 55 and flushes underflow) and qemu (which computes them) differ by
design rather than by defect.

  ./run_fpdiff.sh <first_seed> <last_seed> [ops]     # SIM=<verilated tb>

FPDIFF_MODE selects coverage:
  unset   extended precision, all rounding modes, all instructions
  arith   all precisions and modes, arithmetic only (no register FMOVE)
  all     everything -- diverges, see below

qemu 11 deviation found 2026-08-04: **qemu does not apply the FPCR rounding
precision (bits 7:6) to FMOVE into a floating-point register.**  A plain
FMOVE is an arithmetic instruction for rounding purposes and must round its
result like any other; WinUAE's reference `fp_move(a, b, prec)` sets the
rounding precision around the move, and AP040 does the same.  Minimal
repro: with FPCR=$40 (single, round to nearest), FMOVE.X of the extended
value $3FFF/$8000000000000FFF gives $8000000000000000 on AP040 and on real
040 silicon, but $8000000000000FFF on qemu.  Because the wrong operand then
feeds every later instruction, a single such FMOVE makes an entire random
program diverge; that is why reduced precision is only generated in the
modes above.  AP040's behaviour is regression-covered by t_fpu.s tests
88-92 instead.

With that one case excluded, AP040 and qemu agree exactly: arithmetic
across all four rounding modes and all three precisions (FPDIFF_MODE=arith)
and the default mode both match on every seed tried.

MMU differential (gen_mmudiff.py / run_mmudiff.sh)
--------------------------------------------------
Random three-level translation setups inside the 64K the testbench models:
pages 0-5 identity (code, evidence window, stack/tables, result arrays),
pages 6-11 randomly identity / remapped into pages 12-14 / write protected,
page 15 left alone because it holds the testbench protocol registers.  The
program enables translation, runs random translated reads, writes and PTEST
probes, disables translation and copies the evidence into the compared
window: read-back values, the page table itself (whose U/M history bits the
hardware updates during the walks), the root and pointer descriptors, and
the MMUSR results.

  ./run_mmudiff.sh <first_seed> <last_seed> [ops]    # SIM=<verilated tb>

Writes are never generated against a write-protected page and all mappings
are resident, so no access error is taken: fault frames are format $7 on
AP040 and a different shape on qemu, and t_mmu.s covers them instead.

qemu 11 deviations found 2026-08-04, both worked around in the generator:

  1. qemu does not flush its internal TLB when TC is written, so stale
     translations survive enabling *and disabling* the MMU.  Real 040
     hardware flushes both ATCs on a TC write (t_mmu.s test 37 asserts
     this on AP040), so the generator issues PFLUSHA after every TC write
     to keep the comparison meaningful.
  2. qemu never sets the M (modified) history bit in a page descriptor.
     AP040 sets it on the first write to a page, as the 68040 must, which
     also shows up in MMUSR; t_mmu.s tests 5-7 cover it.  The generator
     clears the M bit in the evidence copies so the rest still compares.

With those two accounted for, AP040 and qemu agree exactly on translation,
write protection, the U history bits on all three table levels, and PTEST
MMUSR results across every seed tried (20 seeds x 24 operations).

Further qemu 11 FP deviations found 2026-08-04 while closing the FPU gaps:

  3. Reduced rounding precision narrows the significand, NOT the exponent
     range.  softfloat's SOFTFLOAT_68K path checks overflow/underflow
     against 0x7FFE for every rounding precision, and its roundMask only
     trims mantissa bits.  AP040 originally clamped overflow/underflow at
     the single/double exponent boundaries; the FP differential caught it
     and it is fixed (t_fpu.s covers the rounding, and FPDIFF_MODE=unfl
     exercises tiny operands at reduced precision).
  4. Extended subnormals: an exponent field of zero scales by 2^-16382,
     i.e. softfloat normalizes a subnormal to exponent (1 - shiftCount)
     and packs an underflowed result by shifting right (1 - exponent).
     qemu encodes such results one power of two too large, and does not
     set UNFL.  Minimal repro: 2^-16380 * 2^-13 = 2^-16393 gives exponent
     0 with significand $0010000000000000 on AP040 (and per softfloat),
     $0020000000000000 on qemu.  t_fpu.s tests 93-99 pin AP040's encoding.
